WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.800
Ask why, know your numbers, and stay safe.

2
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:08.340
This is the video for Module 1 of Negotiation Skills for Business and Life.

3
00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:15.940
A negotiation is any conversation where people who need something from each other try to reach an agreement.

4
00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:22.140
Buying fabric, a customer asking for a discount, rent with a landlord, pay with an employer.

5
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:29.170
You negotiate at home too, about school fees, chores, or who travels for a family event.

6
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:36.200
It is not a talent some people are born with. It is a set of habits you can learn and practise.

7
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.360
The most useful idea in this course comes from the book Getting to Yes.

8
00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:54.210
People usually argue over positions. A position is the specific thing someone says they want, like I will only pay 40000 naira. When you only argue positions, one person must lose.

9
00:00:55.460 --> 00:01:02.260
Behind every position sits an interest. That is the need, worry or goal that answers the question why.

10
00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:13.240
A supplier who demands full payment upfront may be worried about customers who never pay. When you understand interests, you can often find an option that satisfies both.

11
00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:20.480
Here are the four principles in plain words. One. Be soft on the person and firm on the issue.

12
00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:25.020
Two. Ask why, listen, and share your own interests too.

13
00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:33.460
Three. Before deciding, list several possible deals. Different payment timing, a bigger quantity, an added service.

14
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:41.380
Four. Use fair standards both sides can check, like current market prices or the cost of materials.

15
00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:52.750
Here is a worked example. Mrs Bello, a school administrator, wants 60 bags. Each bag costs Ngozi about 2800 naira to make.

16
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:05.730
Mrs Bello offers 3000 a bag. Ngozi's normal price is 4500. The gap is 90000 naira on the whole order. That is a fight of positions.

17
00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:11.650
So Ngozi asks, can you tell me a bit about the budget and what the bags are for?

18
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:21.920
Now she learns the interest. The parents' association approved 200000 in total, and the bags must carry the school logo and be ready in three weeks.

19
00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:33.430
Ngozi offers three options. Mrs Bello chooses 60 bags without the inner pocket at 3300 each, so 198000.

20
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.450
Ngozi keeps a profit of about 30000, and gets a repeat customer.

21
00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:46.040
Many women lose money not because they cannot talk, but because they never decided their numbers in advance.

22
00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:54.240
Your BATNA is your best alternative to a negotiated agreement. It is not a wish. It is a real option you could take tomorrow.

23
00:02:55.490 --> 00:03:01.340
Your walk-away point comes from your BATNA. Never accept a deal worse than your best alternative.

24
00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.450
Your target is the good, realistic result you are aiming for.

25
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:17.000
And the ZOPA, the zone of possible agreement, is the range between the seller's walk-away point and the buyer's. A deal can only happen inside it.

26
00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:21.320
Halima's neighbour will still buy the freezer this month.

27
00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:27.460
So her BATNA is 120000 naira, and that is her walk-away point.

28
00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:35.750
She checks similar freezers online and with two repairers, then sets her target at 155000.

29
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:46.190
Chinedu privately will pay up to 145000. So the ZOPA is 120000 to 145000.

30
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:56.510
Halima opens at 160000. Chinedu offers 125000. They settle at 140000.

31
00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:06.010
Halima earned 20000 more than her BATNA, because she knew her numbers and did not panic at a low first offer. Keep your walk-away point private.

32
00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:15.730
Some negotiations carry risk beyond money. Meet strangers in public or familiar places, and take someone with you if you can.

33
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:27.490
Never pay a deposit to someone you have not verified. Check payment alerts in your bank app, not a screenshot. Never share your BVN, card PIN or one-time codes.

34
00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.810
And saying, let me think about it and get back to you tomorrow, is always acceptable.

35
00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:41.760
To remember. A position is what someone asks for, an interest is why. Be soft on the person and firm on the problem.

36
00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:50.690
Never accept a deal worse than your BATNA, and keep your walk-away point private. And verify people and payments before money changes hands.

37
00:04:51.940 --> 00:05:02.740
Now your turn. Find the interests behind a real disagreement. Write your numbers for a negotiation coming up. Then notice your own style and write three safety rules.

38
00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.030
Continue with the lessons below.
